<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Acute viral gastroenteritis with mild dehydration (A08.4)</content>. A household outbreak of vomiting and diarrhea, most likely norovirus, with mild dehydration (postural tachycardia, dry mouth, less urine) but able to take sips. No blood, high fever, severe pain, or recent antibiotics, so no stool tests or antibiotics. Oral rehydration is appropriate; his baseline QTc of 404 ms allows a short course of ondansetron with sertraline. [S]<list><item>Ondansetron 4 mg dissolving tablet every 8 hours as needed for vomiting, for up to three days.</item><item>Oral rehydration solution in small, frequent sips, then a normal diet as tolerated.</item><item>Rise slowly and sit or lie down when lightheaded.</item><item>Return or go to the emergency department for vomiting that prevents any fluids for 12 hours, blood in the stool, severe pain, confusion, or fainting.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Functional/dissociative seizures (F44.5)</content>. One typical event during the illness, provoked by feeling faint and unwell. A functional seizure during an acute illness is common and does not change the diagnosis; no antiseizure medicine is needed. [S]<list><item>Continue his grounding steps and the diary.</item><item>Lie down when lightheaded to tell faintness from an event.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Major depressive disorder, single episode, moderate (F32.1)</content>. Two missed doses of sertraline from vomiting; no suicidal thoughts. [S]<list><item>Restart sertraline 50 mg once he keeps fluids down; missing two days is unlikely to cause discontinuation symptoms.</item><item>Safety plan, 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline, and clinic number confirmed.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, depression, bipolar, and mood disorders monograph (2026); Hingray C, et al. Functional/dissociative seizures: Proposal for a new diagnostic label and definition by the ILAE task force. Epilepsia 2025;66(11):4162-4182 (PMID 40884444) (2025); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Assessment and Management of Patients at Risk for Suicide (2024)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Three-month review as booked; sooner for the warning signs above. Next scheduled visit 1774-09-24: three-month review after completing psychotherapy: events, mood, driving, and his studies. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
